1
00:00:01,900 --> 00:00:03,720
On paper, it works.

2
00:00:06,950 --> 00:00:09,450
Field trial. ADME Interop.

3
00:00:10,050 --> 00:00:16,384
A 15% project. Can AI agents do real work on a real, shared platform?

4
00:00:18,984 --> 00:00:22,996
So start with one question. Who has access right now?

5
00:00:24,396 --> 00:00:29,752
This is the access guide for one ADME instance, shared by about a dozen companies.

6
00:00:30,252 --> 00:00:35,816
Every time someone is invited or gets a grant, it's rebuilt, and sent to everyone again.

7
00:00:36,316 --> 00:00:38,136
And again.

8
00:00:39,136 --> 00:00:44,700
It's one platform, and every company on it tests its systems against the same data.

9
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:52,562
The guide does real work. How to connect. What each role can do. What a 401 means.

10
00:00:53,062 --> 00:00:55,850
Then three pages that are never true for long.

11
00:00:56,350 --> 00:01:03,066
Who has access, who can reach which seismic subproject, and which object id is which person.

12
00:01:04,666 --> 00:01:10,598
Those pages come from Azure CLI calls, Graph queries and scripts, put together by hand.

13
00:01:11,198 --> 00:01:15,226
By the time the guide lands in an inbox, it's already behind.

14
00:01:15,826 --> 00:01:20,926
So when a partner asks if they have access yet, someone stops and checks by hand.

15
00:01:22,626 --> 00:01:26,622
So why not point an AI model at the APIs and let it answer?

16
00:01:26,922 --> 00:01:30,478
Because the platform makes it easy to be confidently wrong.

17
00:01:30,978 --> 00:01:36,718
A search missing one parenthesis comes back 200. Zero results, no error.

18
00:01:37,218 --> 00:01:41,968
Count every record of a big kind, and it stops at 10,000 without saying so.

19
00:01:42,468 --> 00:01:45,840
And no model should invite anyone without a dry run.

20
00:01:46,740 --> 00:01:50,800
A smarter model isn't the fix. It needs better ground to stand on.

21
00:01:52,300 --> 00:01:54,120
So, three pieces.

22
00:01:54,620 --> 00:02:04,872
A workbench for agents, running on one machine. That's Keelson. It keeps the record, runs workflows with approval gates, and serves its tools over MCP.

23
00:02:05,372 --> 00:02:13,304
A rib, bolted onto it, that knows this ADME instance: its people, its data, its seismic subprojects.

24
00:02:13,804 --> 00:02:20,904
And an MCP server that knows OSDU itself, so an agent gets bounded answers that say how sure they are.

25
00:02:21,204 --> 00:02:25,138
One tool runs today. Four more are in work.

26
00:02:25,938 --> 00:02:29,566
Next, the guide comes apart, page by page.

27
00:02:31,166 --> 00:02:33,948
Page four. Who has access.

28
00:02:34,451 --> 00:02:41,535
Here it's measured, not written. Thirty-two people from twelve companies, and how far each one got.

29
00:02:42,037 --> 00:02:47,185
Twenty-nine accepted. Only three have made a data call. That's who to follow up with.

30
00:02:47,685 --> 00:02:51,009
Measured a minute ago, not on the day the PDF went out.

31
00:02:52,414 --> 00:02:57,354
Page three is troubleshooting. So, why does Rachel get a 401?

32
00:02:57,854 --> 00:03:06,076
The rib runs the platform's checks in the order it applies them. She's an editor, but she isn't in users@, so every call stops there.

33
00:03:06,579 --> 00:03:09,783
The fix is one change, and there's a note ready to send her.

34
00:03:11,191 --> 00:03:15,637
Page five. Who can reach which seismic subproject.

35
00:03:16,138 --> 00:03:22,902
Thirteen subprojects. Two are open to anyone with a data role. One has no members at all.

36
00:03:23,404 --> 00:03:29,432
Give Lena viewer on alpha, and it's a dry run first: the exact calls, and what will change.

37
00:03:29,934 --> 00:03:32,788
Nothing is written until someone applies it.

38
00:03:34,194 --> 00:03:38,894
And the guide never said what data the instance holds. The tab does.

39
00:03:39,396 --> 00:03:47,600
1.28 million records under four legal tags. One expires in twenty-two days, four days before the pilot pass ends.

40
00:03:49,400 --> 00:03:55,300
Agents propose. People approve. And every answer says how sure it is.

41
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:00,302
That's the alpha. The next revision lands when the next piece works.

42
00:04:04,702 --> 00:04:10,842
Field trials. On paper, it works. In the field, it has to.

